\f0\b\fs45\fsmilli22667 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Identify, Predict, Actuate: Observation-Calibrated Safe Capacity for Small VPS Hosts under Censored Demand and Phase Structure\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf2 Kaixiang Nie\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf3 \strokec3 Fujian Chuanzheng Communications College, Fuzhou, China\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf3 Corresponding author: Kaixiang Nie \'b7 email: me@niekaixiang.com\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 \strokec2 Abstract\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Small VPS providers pack many cheap, mostly-idle, occasionally-bursty tenants onto few physical cores; oversubscription is their business model. We recast safe oversubscription as three ordered problems whose bottleneck is observational capability. First, quota censoring hides demand: the point estimate equals the running-stretch mean, exact only when running and throttled stretch means agree; a conditional floor holds under continuous saturation; and from aggregate counters alone no estimator can be correct for all workloads, since two processes can produce identical observations with different demand. Across 180 cycles, throttle-corrected estimates track the truth while naive quota-release decisions violate the SLO in all 30 independent rounds. Second, capacity is set by phase structure, not average utilization: at the same 20% duty, random-phase and aligned fleets differ 3.5x in safe capacity (28 versus 8 seats at customer P5 >= 70%), and a 35%-busy set collapsing while a 98%-busy set stays safe shows that safety cannot be read off utilization. Third, the sampling rate must resolve the bursts it prices. Together these capabilities form an observation set that a risk-calibrated admission controller implements: in the 14 evaluated scenarios it admitted 16-20 tenants under randomized phases and stopped at eight under aligned phases while meeting the customer-quality bar, whereas baseline policies either oversold (customer P5 collapsing to 16%) or wasted 2-3x capacity (these are the original policy-comparison figures; the integrated probation variant is reported separately and is more conservative, Section 7.4.2). All claims rest on mass repeats, two testbeds, read-only telemetry from production hosts we operate, and released artifacts.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 Keywords\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 oversubscription; admission control; censored demand; phase structure; tail latency; cloud resource management\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 1 Introduction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 1.1 Background\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 In the cheap-VPS market, sellers survive by packing many low-utilization customer VMs onto a small number of physical cores. We observed two real production hosts (anonymized): one carries 64 running customer VMs (70 vCPUs configured) on 4 physical cores / 8 logical threads; another (50 cores) configures 285 VMs with 276 running (8.3x vCPU, 2.0x memory oversubscription). For small providers oversubscription is not an optional optimization but the business model: "1 core, 2 GB" is cheap precisely because most tenants are idle most of the time. The hard part is not "whether to oversubscribe" but three ordered technical problems: 
\f0\b can we see it (identify), can we predict it (predict), can we push it (actuate)
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 1.2 Three Problems\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Identify: demand is censored, and aggregates cannot fully recover it.
\f1\b0  CPU quotas are standard; once a tenant hits its cap, its "usage" no longer represents demand \'97 a tenant throttled at 50% while wanting 100% looks like an idle one. We further formalize this: from aggregate (usage, throttle-fraction) counters alone, 
\f0\b no estimator that sees only these counters can be correct for all workloads
\f1\b0  (exact non-identifiability \'97 identical observations, different demand; stronger than statistical unbiasedness; construction in Section 3.3.1); what can be certified is a point estimate of the running-stretch mean plus a shape-free floor $q(1+t)$. The identifiability boundary is therefore stated explicitly rather than assumed away.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Predict: capacity is set by phase structure, not average utilization.
\f1\b0  With the same per-tenant duty (20%), 64 tenants in random phase safely sell 28 seats (P5 \uc0\u8805  70% bar; fine-scanned boundary [28,32)) while fully aligned tenants sell only 8 (boundary [8,10)); and a 35%-busy synchronized set collapses while a 98%-busy random set stays safe \'97 
\f0\b safety is not identifiable from utilization
\f1\b0 . Phase structure is measurable from one-second per-tenant history (phase profiles) and can even be arranged at provisioning time (phase scattering).\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Actuate: efficiency levers must be split into invisible gains and tenant-visible regressions.
\f1\b0  Core isolation, KSM dedup, and free-page reporting are invisible to tenants; quotas, IO caps, and zram carry explicit prices. The product space of a small provider is exactly "how far density can be pushed without reducing tenant-visible resources".\
\pard\pardeftab720\li480\fi-480\ri480\sa213\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf3 \strokec3 Observation design: the three problems share one bottleneck \'97 observational capability. Three capabilities (censoring identification / phase resolution / time resolution) are each necessary (Section 3.6) and jointly sufficient for our target workload family (an empirical statement); the Section 7 comparison attributes each baseline's failure to the capability it lacks.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 \strokec2 1.3 Approach Overview\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Figure 1 summarizes the study pipeline and the observation set. Two self-built testbeds make the problems verifiable: a 
\f0\b nested PVE bed
\f1\b0  (4 vCPU / 19.5 GiB, 16 tenants) and a 
\f0\b bare-metal bed
\f1\b0  (2x Xeon 8259CL, 96 threads / 991 GiB, 64-VM fleet; isolation claim verified against SMT/NUMA topology), anchored by read-only telemetry from two production hosts. Around the three pillars:\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
