\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 2 Related Work\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 2.1 Oversubscription and Admission in Cloud Platforms\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Oversubscription is central to cloud resource efficiency. AUDIBLE [1] (ASPLOS 2024) controls server capacity violations with per-tenant empirical distributions combined convolutionally, substantially improving packing at hyperscale; Coach [2] (ASPLOS 2025) exploits long-term temporal patterns across all resources and monitors/mitigates contention; Hestia [3] (SIGCOMM 2026) aggregates workloads by temporal complementarity using a volatility metric (MCV) to stabilize oversubscription ratios. All of these assume long, rich, correlatable history and target platform-side capacity accounting.\
Our setting exposes two blind spots of that line for small providers. First, 
\f0\b synchronization
\f1\b0 : temporal complementarity works precisely because loads are out of phase; when tenant loads synchronize (hourly jobs, batch deployments, attacks), aggregation aligns the spikes \'97 our one-minute periodic spike timeline is direct evidence. Second, the 
\f0\b observability gap
\f1\b0 : hyperscalers aggregate over billing windows, whereas small providers protect themselves with hard quotas; once a quota binds, "usage" becomes a censored observation, and Section 5 shows that decisions based on it systematically overestimate headroom (3.3\'d7 underestimate of true demand).\
On the admission side, Kerveros [4] (OSDI 2023) reports production capacity bookkeeping and buffering in a large public cloud; ProtoHAIL/ProtoRAIL [5] learns risk-aware adaptive vCPU oversubscription ratios via prototypical imitation learning with human-in-the-loop; conformal demand envelopes for GPU clusters [7] (2024) turn oversubscription into calibrated risk envelopes; SCROOGEVM [6] adjusts oversubscription ratios based on detected host quiescence; RSR [8] (2026) uses calibrated interval prediction to size individual VMs. These works are strong on prediction and calibration, but their evaluation targets are platform-side capacity violations or single-VM sizing, not 
\f0\b which control mechanism actually protects business tail latency on a small host
\f1\b0 . Our controlled comparisons of CPU quotas, CPU weights, core isolation, memory double-gates, and IO caps/weights answer exactly that, yielding the "isolation first, hard caps second, no soft priorities" conclusion.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 2.2 Resource-Control Primitives and Scheduling\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 cgroup v2 supplies the entire control surface we use: 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 cpu.max
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  (bandwidth quota and period), 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 cpu.weight
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 , 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 memory.high
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 /
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 memory.max
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 , 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 memory.swap.max
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 , 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 io.max
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 /
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 io.weight
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 /
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 io.latency
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 , and PSI [16-18]. The kernel documentation defines the semantics but leaves the 
\f0\b combined behavior under multi-tenant oversubscription
\f1\b0  open. Our measurements add operationally important facts: block-IO accounting may bind to the whole-disk device rather than the mounted partition; 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 io.weight
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  only distributes bandwidth when an IO scheduler such as BFQ honors it (the host default 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 none
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  makes it a no-op); CPU quotas protect averages while manufacturing pulse trains that hurt tail latency; and even 100\'d7 CPU weight does not protect short-request tails on a saturated host.\
On the KVM scheduling side, the community has long studied lock-holder preemption and vCPU spinning under oversubscription [18] (KVM Forum 2021); a December 2025 kernel patch series (sched/kvm: Semantics-aware vCPU scheduling for oversubscribed KVM) continues improving this with a vCPU debooster and IPI-aware directed yield. These mechanism-level results align with our observations: oversubscribed-host tail latency is not an average-value problem, and the reliable way to bring tails back under control is to 
\f0\b remove the critical workload from the contention set (isolation)
\f1\b0  rather than to fine-tune priorities within it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 2.3 Industrial Practice and Tools\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 The Kubernetes ecosystem expresses differentiated policies as "overcommit classes": Inditex's k8s-overcommit-operator [10] rewrites resource requests per workload label, and OpenShift's ClusterResourceOverride [11] controls node density through request/limit ratios; KubeVirt [12] exposes a global 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 cpuAllocationRatio
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  and its community still requests per-VM overcommit overrides (issue #15710: opened 2025-09, closed unfixed 2026-02), showing that per-customer oversubscription tiers remain poorly supported in practice. Research prototypes SweetSpotVM/SlackVM [13] demonstrate multi-level oversubscription templates with local schedulers; Calabi [14]'s host resource management stacks Gold/Silver/Bronze slices, guest/emulator CPU pools and pinning, with KSM/zram for memory oversubscription. Our work complements these artifacts with quantitative boundaries: for instance, on the memory path, compression/swap is exactly the failure path our measurements recommend keeping off by default (uncapped trials OOM-killed two tenants; capped trials drained host swap), whereas the double-gate (memory.high + memory.swap.max=0) bounds oversubscription without killing tenants or polluting host swap.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 2.4 Measurement Methodology\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 On methodology, Metior [9] (ASE 2021) warns that adjacent time-series samples are not independent and that quantile comparisons must be estimated in time blocks or independent runs. All our controlled comparisons use a fixed SLO (P99 \uc0\u8804  10 ms), identical arrival sequences, open-loop generators (logging planned/actual send times, timeouts, and errors), and multiple replicates of every key conclusion (five rounds for the knee, seven for censoring, four synthesis passes); results that do not meet this standard are explicitly labeled preliminary.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 2.5 Positioning\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Mechanistic distinction from temporal-pattern work (explicit boundary).
\f1\b0  Coach [2] and Hestia [3] also exploit temporal structure, but their mechanisms presuppose 
\f0\b mobility/arrangability
\f1\b0 : Coach relies on long-term prediction and VM types with guaranteed/oversubscribed partitions; Hestia aggregates complementary workloads into groups and schedules them (with control-plane budget). Our regime (one or few small hosts, no migration freedom, no long history, quota-censored demand) forbids these mechanisms; PPC is therefore a 
\f0\b read-only, measurement-driven
\f1\b0  estimator whose new mechanism is (i) a 
\f0\b joint block bootstrap
\f1\b0  over per-tenant 1-s phase profiles (convolution on the measured joint structure, not on marginals), and (ii) a decision rule pairing the Section 3.3.1 identifiability boundary with "point estimate + tail bar + zero-over-admission". The two settings therefore ask different questions: prior work schedules workloads to smooth volatility, whereas this paper predicts safe capacity and enforces it online when scheduling is unavailable.\
We claim neither a new learning algorithm nor new prediction theory. This is a 
\f0\b measurement-driven systems study
\f1\b0  for small providers: it decomposes "multi-resource oversubscription with multiple control levers" into separately comparable experimental units, quantifies which folk practices hold and which do not under short history, censored demand, and mixed bursty loads, and delivers a combined-stress-validated admission and shaping configuration. The relationship to hyperscale work is complementary: our hosts are smaller, observability is worse, and the decision population is cheaper \'97 exactly the operating conditions of a large share of the global low-cost VPS market.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 3 System Model and Problem Definition\
\pard\pardeftab720\li480\fi-480\ri480\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs28 \cf3 \strokec3 This chapter formalizes only what the later experiments actually use; no assumptions are introduced without evaluation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 \strokec2 3.1 Platform and Tenant Model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Consider a single multi-tenant host (the typical small-provider setup) with $C$ logical CPUs, $M$ bytes of physical memory, and one shared block device (throttling and accounting keyed to the whole-disk device). Two classes of workloads run on it:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Sensitive services
\f1\b0 : $s$ lightweight service VMs , e.g., status monitoring, gateway, static serving whose end-to-end latency must meet a service-level objective. We instrument one HTTP and one Redis service.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Customer VMs (tenants)
\f1\b0 : $n$ oversubscribed customer machines (typically 1 vCPU, a few GiB of memory) \'97 the revenue source. Tenant load is characterized by a class: duty cycle $\\rho$ (fraction of time working), period $P$, phase $\\phi$ (aligned or randomized), resident memory, and IO intensity. We cover four classes: pegged ($\\rho\{=\}1$), bursty ($\\rho\{\\approx\}1/3$), idle ($\\rho\{\\approx\}1/10$), and synchronized (aligned phase).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Each tenant VM $i$ can be constrained by a control vector $a_i = (\\mathrm\{pin\}_i, q_i, w_i, g_i, b_i, \\mathrm\{swapmax\}_i)$, all through the cgroup v2 interface [15]: core set, CPU quota (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 cpu.max
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 ), CPU weight (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 cpu.weight
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 ), memory soft limit (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 memory.high
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 ), block-IO cap (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 io.max
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 ), and per-cgroup swap limit (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 memory.swap.max
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 ). Controls never change the sold configuration (no migration, no down-sizing), matching the operational envelope of small providers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 3.2 Service-Level Objective and Metrics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Service SLO.
\f1\b0  Within an evaluation window of length $W$, the $P_\{99\}$ of request completion latency must satisfy $P_\{99\} \\le L$. We use $W \\in [30, 120]$ s depending on scenario and $L = 10$ ms; thresholds are fixed before and never adjusted after results. A window that violates is counted as a 
\f0\b violation window
\f1\b0 . Requests are injected at a fixed rate by an open-loop generator that records planned send time, actual send time, and completion; timeouts (>2 s) and errors are counted separately and excluded from latency quantiles.\

\f0\b Customer-side experience.
\f1\b0  The work rate of a tenant is its measurable busy-loop iteration rate (it/s), compared against the unconstrained full-speed baseline (roughly 55\'9675 M it/s depending on load shape); this metric expresses 
\f0\b where the cost lands
\f1\b0 .\

\f0\b Host-side evidence.
\f1\b0  Per-cgroup 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 cpu.stat
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 usage_usec
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 , 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 nr_throttled
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 , 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 throttled_usec
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 ), 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 memory.current
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 /
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 memory.events
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 , 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 io.stat
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 , plus host-level 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 MemAvailable
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 /swap, 3-second CPU busy fraction and PSI pressure counters [16].\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 3.3 Demand, Censoring, and the Observation Model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 The 
\f0\b true demand
\f1\b0  $d_i(t)$ of tenant $i$ is the CPU it would consume without quota and without contention truncation. The 
\f0\b observable usage
\f1\b0  satisfies\
$$u_i(t) \\;\\le\\; \\min\\big(d_i(t),\\; q_i\\big),$$\
truncated either by an explicit quota ($q_i$ binding) or by contention (all tenants shrink proportionally when the host saturates). Both hide demand from a usage dashboard; we focus on quota censoring and detect it via 
\f0\b throttle evidence
\f1\b0 :\
$$\\text\{censored\}_i(t) \\;=\\; \\mathbf\{1\}\\big[\\Delta\\,\\mathrm\{throttled\\_usec\}_i(t) > \\epsilon \\;\\vee\\; \\Delta\\,\\mathrm\{nr\\_throttled\}_i(t) > 0 \\big],$$\
i.e., the tenant was actually throttled in the window. Two estimators follow:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 naive
\f1\b0 : $\\hat d_i^\{\\text\{naive\}\} = u_i$ (dashboards take usage as demand);\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 censoring-aware (empirical estimator)
\f1\b0 : if $\\text\{censored\}_i = 1$, with throttle fraction $t_i$ over the window, take $\\hat d_i = \\min(\\text\{vCPU\},\\, u_i/(1-t_i))$. This formula assumes the tenant would keep burning at its unthrottled rate during throttled time: it is near-unbiased for 
\f0\b saturated
\f1\b0  demand (our primary workload family; ground-truth error \uc0\u8804 9% in Section 7.3.2), 
\f0\b over-estimates bursty demand
\f1\b0  (counterexample in the unit tests: true demand 0.3, quota 0.2 \uc0\u8594  estimate 1.0), and may under-estimate intermittent saturation. It is therefore an 
\f0\b empirical estimator to be characterized per workload family, not a distribution-free bound
\f1\b0 ; "at least its full vCPU" remains the conservative fallback. If $\\text\{censored\}_i = 0$, use $u_i$ \'97 note $u_i$ is always a (weak) lower bound on demand; the absence of throttling only rules out quota censoring, not contention shrinkage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 History length
\f1\b0  enters the same way: a newly arrived tenant has $T_\{\\text\{hist\}\} = 0$ and admits only class priors or conservative bounds; the cold-start discussion builds on this (a dedicated experiment is listed as future work).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa133\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 3.3.1 Identifiability of Censoring: What Counters Can and Cannot Recover\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Observation model and assumptions.
\f1\b0  Window $W$ spanning several quota periods $P$; quota $q$ (cores); budget $qP$ per period. Let $u$ = mean usage, $t$ = throttled-time fraction, $\\bar d$ = window-mean demand, $\\overline\{d\}_\{\\text\{run\}\}/\\overline\{d\}_\{\\text\{thr\}\}$ = mean demand inside the running / throttled stretches. Assumptions: (A1) single cgroup, work-conserving, no external steal; (A2) the budget is exhausted within the window (else no throttle evidence); (A3) $W \\gg P$, ignoring period-boundary effects. 
\f0\b Caveat:
\f1\b0  
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 throttled_usec
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  counts the cgroup's throttled state in real CFS and may include slack inside throttled windows; the identities below are a bandwidth (budget-exhaustion) model, and the residual versus the implementation is reported at the end.\

\f0\b Proposition 1 (what the point estimate really is, and when it is exact).
\f1\b0  When the budget is exhausted, total delivery in the window is $uW = qW$, all of it inside the unthrottled stretch; hence$$\\hat d := \\frac\{q\}\{1-t\} \\;=\\; \\overline\{d\}_\{\\text\{run\}\}\\qquad(\\text\{mean demand over the running stretch\}),$$and under $d(t)\\ge q$ throughout (no idle slack) we have the 
\f0\b bias identity
\f1\b0 $$\\bar d - \\hat d \\;=\\; t\\,\\big(\\overline\{d\}_\{\\text\{thr\}\} - \\overline\{d\}_\{\\text\{run\}\}\\big).$$The 
\f0\b exactness condition is $\\overline\{d\}_\{\\text\{thr\}\} = \\overline\{d\}_\{\\text\{run\}\}$
\f1\b0  (equal means over the throttled and running stretches) \'97 
\f0\b constant demand is sufficient but not necessary
\f1\b0 . 
\f2\i Non-constant example:
\f1\i0  $q\{=\}0.3$, one 1 s period, demand 0.6 (first 0.5 s) / 0.4 (0.25 s) / 0.8 (0.25 s): the budget is exhausted at 0.5 s, $t\{=\}0.5$, and $\\hat d = 0.3/0.5 = 0.6 = \\bar d$ although demand is plainly not constant. In general: peaks inside the running stretch followed by decay (the typical burst) produce 
\f0\b over-estimation
\f1\b0 ; demand rising during throttle (low-then-high) produces 
\f0\b under-estimation
\f1\b0 ; the sign is shape-dependent and not identifiable from aggregates.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Counterexamples in both directions (idealized model).
\f1\i0  
\f0\b Over:
\f1\b0  $q\{=\}0.5$, $P\{=\}1$ s, demand 1.0 for the first 0.5 s then 0.5; budget is exhausted at 0.5 s, $t\{=\}0.5$, $\\hat d = 1.0 > \\bar d = 0.75$. 
\f0\b Under:
\f1\b0  $q\{=\}0.5$, demand 0.5 for 0.9 s then 1.0; budget exhausts at 0.95 s, $t\{=\}0.05$, $\\hat d = 0.526 < \\bar d = 0.55$.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Proposition 2 (conditional lower bound, under $d \\ge q$).
\f1\b0  If $d(t) \\ge q$ throughout the window (continuous saturation, no slack), then$$\\bar d \\;\\ge\\; q\\,(1+t).$$
\f2\i (Proof: the running stretch accumulates exactly $qW$; the throttled stretch contributes at least $q\\,tW$ because $d \\ge q$ there; add and divide by $W$. Equality iff $d \\equiv q$ while throttled. $\\square$)
\f1\i0  
\f0\b Boundary:
\f1\b0  the precondition "$d \\ge q$ throughout" 
\f0\b cannot be verified from aggregate counters
\f1\b0  \'97 it is a property of the target workload family (continuous saturation), not a per-tenant certification. In the system this floor is used only for tenants with continuous throttle evidence (the conservative backstop of Section 3.3); for everyone else we report the point estimate plus uncertainty and claim no bound.\

\f0\b Boundary (counterexample):
\f1\b0  "continuous throttling" and "d>=q throughout" are 
\f0\b not equivalent
\f1\b0  \'97 e.g. q=0.2 with demand 1.0 for the first 20% of each period and 0.1 for the rest: the budget still exhausts every period (t=0.8, constant throttle evidence) yet the true mean is 0.28, 
\f2\i below
\f1\i0  q(1+t)=0.36. The engineering trigger is therefore an 
\f0\b empirical conservative policy
\f1\b0  (its bias direction is over-estimation, i.e. more conservative admission; it cannot cause over-admission) \'97 not a certified bound, whose status remains conditional on d>=q per Proposition 2.\

\f0\b Proposition 3 (aggregate non-identifiability).
\f1\b0  There exist two demand processes with identical $(u,t,q)$ but different window means; no function of $(u,t,q)$ can be correct for all processes (an identifiability impossibility), and the bias sign is not identifiable. 
\f2\i Construction (throttle time explicitly accounted).
\f1\i0  $q\{=\}0.2$, vCPU$=1$, window = one period. Process A: $d \\equiv 5/9 \\approx 0.556$ (never slack; $5/9$ makes $t$ exactly 0.64) \uc0\u8594  budget exhausts at $0.36W$, $t=0.64$, $u=0.2$, mean $5/9 \\approx 0.556$. Process B: $d=1.0$ for 84% of the duration, 0 otherwise \u8594  budget exhausts at $0.2W$, throttled until $0.84W$, $t=0.64$, $u=0.2$, mean $0.84$. Identical observation $(u,t)=(0.2,0.64)$, means differ by $0.284$; any estimator errs by $\\ge 0.142$ on one of them. $\\square$\

\f0\b Practical consequences.
\f1\b0  (i) Aggregate unbiasedness is unattainable \'97 estimators are characterized per workload family; (ii) recovering demand 
\f2\i structure
\f1\i0  requires 
\f2\i time resolution
\f1\i0  (a per-window $t$ sequence or 1-s history) \'97 the motivation for the phase-profiled aggregation in Section 5: 
\f0\b amplitude from throttle evidence (with a conservative floor), structure from time history
\f1\b0 ; (iii) under-estimation (the unsafe direction) needs an explicit backstop: for continuously saturated tenants the design uses $\\hat d = \\mathrm\{vCPU\}$.\

\f0\b Empirical characterization.
\f1\b0  Saturated family: \uc0\u8804 9% across three quota levels (6.3c); the v2 batch (90 cycles = 450 tenant-windows incl. 90 offline): throttle detection + vCPU backstop gives exactly 1.00; the 360 online windows average $t = 0.470$ and the bare formula gives 0.943 (\u8722 5.7%) \'97 precisely the residual between Proposition 1's model and real CFS accounting (period boundaries, throttled-state timing including slack), removed by the backstop. The bursty family exposes the other side (backstop reports 1.0 for a true 0.3 \'97 Section 3.3), deliberately conservative; the intermittent family's under-estimation is covered by the no-double-promise + pause monitors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 3.4 Decision Problems\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 The system makes two kinds of decisions over an arrival sequence:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Admission
\f1\b0 : for each arriving tenant, use the currently visible evidence (history, throttle evidence, host pressure, latest service-tail windows) to output allow/defer with a reason; batch arrivals require reservation accounting so the same spare capacity is not promised twice.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Shaping
\f1\b0 : choose control parameters $a_i$ for admitted tenants, and decide on "release/expand" requests from capped tenants.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The objective is to maximize admitted volume (count, VM-hours, or configuration-weighted) subject to the service SLO, while reporting costs (customer work-rate loss, throttled-tenant queueing) separately and without hiding them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 3.5 Assumptions and Scope\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Tenant load is not adversarial: our "pegged tenant" is legitimate but extreme (mining, compilation); malicious behavior is out of scope.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No live migration, no killing of tenants, no re-configuring of already-sold resources \'97 these change the service model and are deferred.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The testbed is a nested virtualization environment (Proxmox inside a KVM guest); results reflect the behavior of a topology isomorphic to small-provider production but do not substitute for bare-metal extrapolation.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The 10 ms SLO is our strict default; the evaluation chapter discusses sensitivity across thresholds.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 3.6 Observation Necessity: Three Constructive Counterexamples and the Minimal Observation Set\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 For the decision problems studied in this paper, 
\f0\b the binding constraint is observational capability rather than the capacity algorithm.
\f1\b0  Three constructive results (formal proofs and executable counterexamples in Appendix A), each with measured counterparts here:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Proposition A (censoring impossibility).
\f1\b0  Given only aggregate counters (usage, throttle fraction, quota), no estimator that sees only these counters can be correct for all workloads \'97 two processes can produce 
\f0\b identical observations
\f1\b0  with different demand (construction in Section 3.3.1, Prop. 3). This is an 
\f0\b identifiability
\f1\b0  impossibility (it holds for any deterministic function of the observations) and is stronger than statistical unbiasedness. 
\f2\i Corollary:
\f1\i0  dashboard-driven release/oversell decisions are unfixably dangerous \'97 90/90 independent-round judgments violate (Section 4.3b).\

\f0\b Proposition B1 (capacity is not determined by marginals; construction).
\f1\b0  N tenants each work w seconds per period P (identical per-tenant marginals). Aligned phases give peak concurrency N; random phases give an overlap distribution with mean rho N (rho = w/P). With pool capacity C, the aligned fleet is safe iff N <= C, while the random fleet's capacity is bounded above by C/rho (mean-concurrency necessary condition rho
\f2\i N <= C) \'97 a 
\f4\b capacity ratio of at most 1/rho = 5
\f2\b0  at rho = 0.2 , i.e., random/aligned <= 5. Physical realization (Section 8.4): primary-bar capacities 28 vs 8 (fine-scan brackets [28,32)/[8,10)), with the unsafe fleet reporting 
\f1\i0 lower* utilization (30.4% vs 66.4\'9668.0%).\

\f0\b Proposition B2 (threshold impossibility; theorem).
\f1\b0  Let the monitoring window be L seconds. An aligned fleet's windowed delivered utilization is capped at **U(L) = [w*floor(L/P) + min(L mod P, w)] / L**: the total overlap of an L-second window with the burst windows is at most w
\f2\i floor(L/P) + min(L mod P, w), so delivery is at most C times that overlap. Special cases: for L <= P, U(L) = min(w,L)/L (the 30-s rolling window of the sync experiments: 12/30 = 40%); for L an integer multiple of P, U = rho; for L = kP + r with r > 0, U can slightly exceed rho (e.g. L = 90 s: 24/90 = 26.7%). 
\f4\b Hence any rule "admit while windowed utilization < theta" with theta > U(L) admits unbounded N
\f2\b0  on aligned fleets whose safe capacity is only C. 
\f1\i0 Measured counterpart:* util80 (L=30 s, theta=80% > 40%) sells to the experiment cap (40) in the aligned phase while its criterion reads ~20-40% (Section 7.4.1); the "35%-busy collapse vs 98%-busy safe" inversion is a field instance of this theorem.\

\f0\b Proposition C (sampling insufficiency).
\f1\b0  Let a monitor sample utilization at fixed interval Delta with burst width w < Delta. There exist benign and dangerous fleets whose 
\f0\b sample sequences are identical
\f1\b0  , e.g., all zeros while safe capacities differ \'97 place the bursts between samples. 
\f2\i Evidence level:
\f1\i0  formal construction plus indirect measurement (util80's stop alternates 28 vs 40 across identical runs \'97 sampling-moment sensitive, Section 7.4.1); the 
\f0\b direct experiment
\f1\b0  (fixed phases, sampling interval varied) is listed as future work; this proposition is cited as a construction with indirect evidence only. 
\f2\i Corollary:
\f1\i0  the sampling rate must resolve the burst shape of interest (our online history uses 
\f0\b 1 second
\f1\b0 ; a 12-s aligned burst is one phase-bucket wide).\

