\f0\b\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Observations.
\f1\b0  (i) The knee is stable at 3\uc0\u8594 4: with the fourth pegged neighbor, P50 jumps from 1.9 to 11.0 ms and P99 from 4.9 to 23.8 ms, tightly across five rounds. (ii) Tail-percentile warning precedes the median collapse: P999 already fluctuates at 2\'963 neighbors (14\'9627 ms), one or two levels earlier. (iii) The 4-vs-5 comparison is non-monotonic in P50 (11.0 vs 6.9) \'97 reading averages alone would rank 5 neighbors "better". (iv) Zero timeouts throughout: the service "looks healthy" by error rate or mean.\

\f0\b Interpretation.
\f1\b0  Once the runnable set persistently exceeds the logical core count (C = 4; the fourth pegged tenant plus service and probe threads), short requests begin queueing for scheduling shares; collapse is an event of 
\f0\b instantaneous concurrency crossing the capacity line
\f1\b0 , not of average utilization reaching a threshold (at 3 neighbors the host is already 86% busy yet healthy). This explains why fixed-utilization thresholds fail here and motivates managing instantaneous concurrency in the design.\

\f0\b Boundaries.
\f1\b0  One host, 4 logical cores; neighbors are continuously pegged (worst case); no quotas or isolation; absolute numbers do not extrapolate to other core counts, but the mechanism does not depend on the count.\
