\f0\b\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Observations.
\f1\b0  (i) The 3\uc0\u8594 4 cliff reproduces 
\f0\b round after round
\f1\b0 : 48/48 four-neighbor rounds sit at P99 \uc0\u8805  20.7 ms while 93/98 three-neighbor rounds stay \u8804  12 ms \'97 the two distributions do not overlap (Figure 17 right). (ii) Absolute levels run slightly higher than the first 5-round batch (3n 4.9, 4n 23.8 ms \u8594  7.4/24.4 ms here); the ordering and the cliff position are unchanged. We record the drift; plausible causes include host state after ~40 days online and cache/fragmentation differences between batches. (iii) The verdict is threshold-insensitive: even at a 13.5 ms bar only 7% of three-neighbor rounds would be flagged, while the four-neighbor minimum is 20.7 ms. (iv) 
\f0\b v3 rework:
\f1\b0  L2 stays clean (all \uc0\u8804  8.0 ms; the eps=0 capacity remains 2), but 
\f0\b L3's violation rate moves up sharply \'97 45/50 rounds (median P99 16.0 ms)
\f1\b0  \'97 diverging from the v2 reconstruction (eps~7%; exact Clopper-Pearson 95% interval [78%, 97%] for L3); both batches agree that L4 collapses 100% ([93%, 100%]). So 
\f0\b the eps=0 capacity and the L4 collapse replicate; only L3's risk budget drifts with the host environment
\f1\b0  (same protocol, same 87% busy; absolute drift recorded) \'97 the conservative statement uses the eps=0 capacity of 2.\

\f0\b Boundaries.
\f1\b0  The batch baseline differs from the first batch (absolute drift); the post-mortem level merging uses run logs and is automated in 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 make_v2_figs.py
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 . The v3 rework shows the L3 risk budget is batch-sensitive (7% vs 90% violation across batches at the same busy) \'97 capacity claims use the eps=0 bar (2 neighbors) and per-batch eps values are reported explicitly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

