\f0\b\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \strokec2 Interpretation.
\f1\b0  "Mostly idle" and "clean tail" are different properties: an idle population still has a nonzero probability of several members being awake simultaneously; synchronization (intentional alignment or incidental co-start) turns that probability into a regular event. The realistic production mix is closer to "randomized phases + a few persistent heavy tenants", and the mix of Section 4.1's knee with this result shows that 
\f0\b one persistently pegged tenant plus random overlaps already breaks a strict SLO
\f1\b0 . This motivates the cold-start problem: at arrival time one cannot know whether a newcomer is idle or that pegged tenant.\

\f0\b Boundaries.
\f1\b0  Aligned bursts are synthetic; clustered phases arise from simultaneous first-boot (production tenants boot at dispersed times, so the fully randomized case is the optimistic, realistic one); loads are synthetic duty-cycle loops rather than real applications.\
