\f1\b0 : in 90/90 cycles the usage panel reports 2.00 cores (4 \'d7 0.50) while throttle evidence recovers 4.00 cores \'97 exactly the truth. A half-size quota halves the reported demand, every cycle. (ii) 
\f0\b Service
\f1\b0 : quota-phase HTTP P99 averages 11.4 ms (range 9.1\'9614.6; 98% of cycles already across the 10 ms line); released-phase averages 24.7 ms (range 20.6\'9628.9) with 
\f0\b 90/90 (100%) violations
\f1\b0  \'97 the lowest released-phase cycle is still above the quota phase's 95th percentile.\

\f0\b Causal clarification.
\f1\b0  Two statements must be distinguished: the quota-kept phase already fluctuates around the SLO line (mean 11.4 ms; ~98% of cycles violate), so a release decision 
\f0\b significantly worsens an existing overload condition
\f1\b0  rather than transitioning from compliant to first violation; the causal claim here is estimation error -> wrong release -> worse latency.\

\f0\b Interpretation.
\f1\b0  Same direction as the 7-cycle pilot (5 tenants @30%: naive 1.50 vs aware 5.00), but far harder evidence: the underestimate factor equals the quota fraction exactly, throttle evidence restores the truth, and the naive release decision fails in 30 independent rounds (90 time-correlated cycles, clustered by round). The failure is independent of the quota level (30% and 50% variants agree).\

\f0\b Boundaries.
\f1\b0  As in 4.3; this batch runs 4 tenants (one offline).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 4.4 Reliability of Control Levers (Figures 6\'969)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Five candidate levers are each asked to protect the service tail: CPU quotas, CPU weights, core isolation, memory double-gates, and IO caps (with an IO-weight counterexample).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa133\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 4.4.1 CPU quotas: bound averages, not boost safe packing (Figure 6)\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

