\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 4.4.3 Core isolation: the only lever that survives storms on our testbed (Figures 6, 10)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Pinning services and probe to cores 0\'961 and tenants to cores 2\'963 yields zero violations at 1\'965 pegged neighbors (P99 2.9\'963.2 ms); tenants share the remaining two cores as expected (about 44% of full speed each at five tenants). The combined-stress synthesis (Section 4.5 / 6.5) reproduces this four times. The cost is explicitly relocated to the "economy tier" \'97 protected services no longer compete.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa133\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 4.4.4 Memory: the double gate (Figure 7)\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs25\fsmilli12667 \cf3 \strokec3 \
Figure 7 Memory double gate\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \strokec2 Unbounded growth (10 tenants, progressive ballast): at about 13.8 GB of combined tenant residency the host's 1 GB swap is exhausted and 
\f0\b two customer VMs are OOM-killed
\f1\b0 , with several tenants' SSH unresponsive; services, being small, survive. Adding 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 memory.high
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  (1000/800 MB) eliminates deaths and stabilizes the host, but the reclaim triggered by capped tenants lands almost entirely on anonymous-page swap-out \'97 the small swap drains within seconds and becomes a global bottleneck, with tenants experiencing brief unresponsiveness. Setting 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 memory.swap.max = 0
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  alongside (the double gate) stops swap pollution: oversubscription is throttled in place. Note also: with ballooning disabled, memory a tenant "has touched" returns to the host only on VM restart \'97 dynamic return and the "no compression" stance are an explicit tradeoff.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa133\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 4.4.5 Block IO: hard caps work, the offender pays (Figure 8)\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs25\fsmilli12667 \cf3 \strokec3 \
Figure 8 Block-IO cap (io.max)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \strokec2 Ten tenants running 4K 70/30 random read/write (O_DIRECT) push the host to about 16.5k IOPS and 
\f0\b drag service P99 to 12.1 ms (violating) although services perform almost no disk IO
\f1\b0  (a storm hitting "over the mountain"). Applying 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 io.max = 300 riops / 150 wiops
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  per tenant caps exactly as configured, drops the host to 2.6k IOPS, and returns service P99 to 2.7 ms; the cost is that 
\f0\b capped tenants' read completion P99 rises to ~96 ms
\f1\b0  (queueing at the throttle). Two operational details: cgroup IO accounting keys to the whole-disk device (8:0), not the partition; 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 io.weight
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  is a no-op because the host's IO scheduler is 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 none
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  (no BFQ).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Section boundaries.
\f1\b0  Service VMs being lightweight is a precondition of "isolation works"; the mechanism behind weight failure (host-side packet path / scheduling-credit dynamics) is left as an open question; memory numbers are tied to the 1 GB swap environment.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
