\f0\b\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Reading.
\f1\b0  The phase gate is the aligned-phase safety-critical component (removing it re-creates the 32-seat failure). The two 80%-cap arms differ by four random-phase seats (18 vs 14); 
\f0\b both stops were capacity-bracket deferrals, not guard triggers
\f1\b0  (verified in the decision logs: n = 20 and n = 16 respectively), and each round re-randomizes tenant phases independently, so the difference is dominated by run-to-run phase placement (the show12 band is about +/-25%) - the censoring correction's systematic effect is not separable at one round per arm. Capping admitted tenants at 80% mildly oversells the aligned bracket (8 -> 10, the first failing level).\

\f0\b Dense-sampling confirmation of the boundary (show9, 1 s sampling).
\f1\b0  Re-running the sell ladders at one-second resolution confirms the aligned boundary: level 8 holds customer P5 at 
\f0\b 97.3%
\f1\b0  (per-customer time-P5, median convention) while level 10 collapses to 
\f0\b 47.8%
\f1\b0 ; random 28 holds 
\f0\b 75.7%
\f1\b0  and 30 falls to 
\f0\b 58.3%
\f1\b0 , tightening the dense random boundary to [28,30) (the max-rate convention still reads 90.6% at 32 - the convention sensitivity quantified in Section 8.4). Service P99 stays at 0.70-0.75 ms at every level: the binding constraint is the customer rate, not the service tail.\

\f0\b Guard-metric sensitivity (show10).
\f1\b0  Switching the guard metric from max to median keeps the aligned stop at 8 but pulls the random stop down to 
\f0\b 4
\f1\b0  (pause-monitor triggers on 6/6 checks; P5 = 101.9% at stop): the metric trades random-phase efficiency for conservatism. 
\f0\b Guard semantics (precise):
\f1\b0  the customer guard consumes one number per admitted tenant - the tenant's rate series (six samples, ordered before the service probes so work phases are captured) summarized per tenant by max (largest observed work-phase rate), med or min - and the guard value is the cross-customer 5th percentile of that summary (p5_max / p5_med / p5_min); admission pauses when it falls below 70% (or the service/canary thresholds fire). The primary bar uses p5_max because idle samples are not evidence of denial: a tenant's work capability is evidenced by its work-phase rate, and sustained contention lowers the max too - a full-speed burst cannot survive sustained overload. Median/min conflate design-idle with contention-throttling and false-alarm: in the show10 median arm every pause check in the random phase tripped (6/6 windows; idle samples dilute each tenant's median), stopping at 4 seats, while the aligned phase keeps tenants active and still stops at 8. The 70% and service thresholds are the pre-declared constants of Section 8.4; the 0.9 budget and hysteresis are fixed on validation splits. 
\f0\b Activity-conditioned cross-check (implemented mid-batch, show14).
\f1\b0  To separate design-idle from denial without relying on the max convention, the customer metric is also computed over 
\f0\b active samples only
\f1\b0  - per-customer median over the samples where the workload itself reports a work phase (state flag), excluding design-idle samples, then cross-customer P5 (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 p5_act
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 ). The false-alarm mechanism is visible directly in the logs: windows whose raw p5_med / p5_min read 0.0% (idle-sample dilution) read 102.6% under the activity-conditioned rule, and across the integrated runs 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 p5_act
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  tracks 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 p5_max
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  (101-103% at every safe stop) - the two conventions agree once design-idle samples are excluded. 
\f0\b Detection parity at violating levels (offline, dense ladders).
\f1\b0  Recomputing both conventions on the 1-s dense ladders shows that at violating levels the activity-conditioned metric alarms at least as strongly as p5_max, and earlier: aligned 8 seats read 99.2% / 98.0% (max / act), 10 seats read 86.2% / 
\f0\b 71.7%
\f1\b0 , 12 seats read 75.4% / 
\f0\b 57.9%
\f1\b0 ; random 28 reads 95.1% / 90.6% and 32 reads 90.6% / 
\f0\b 76.7%
\f1\b0 . The activity-conditioned rule tracks sustained denial rather than the best burst - the strict, safe direction for a guard - and it never misses a violation that p5_max catches. 
\f0\b Production observability (honest scope).
\f1\b0  The activity flag used here is the workload's own work-phase indicator, available in experiments; on an opaque host without a guest agent it is not directly observable. The deployment path is therefore two-track: online, the controller keeps p5_max plus throttle evidence and the service/canary guards (all host-side); p5_act is a validation-grade metric, computable in production with an optional guest agent or by host-side activity inference of the same kind the censoring analysis studies (Section 3.3.1), and its online use is future work.\

\f0\b Phase-selective admission (show11, 
\f5\fs28\fsmilli14107 ours_sel
\f0\fs30\fsmilli15333 ).
\f1\b0  The probation mechanism (candidates observed at 5% quota for 40 s before the admission decision) keeps the aligned stop at 8 and raises the random stop to 
\f0\b 24 with no violation
\f1\b0  - the highest safe random-phase stop in the comparison (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 ours_full
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  16, 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 ours_p
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  20), showing that phase evidence acquired before the decision converts into measurable random-phase capacity at equal safety. 
\f0\b Integrated re-validation (show13, 
\f5\fs28\fsmilli14107 ours_int
\f0\fs30\fsmilli15333  = probation + full-chain background reserve and low-history guards).
\f1\b0  End-to-end, the integrated controller stops at 
\f0\b random 12-16 across three arrival orders (14/12/16) and aligned 6 in both repeated orders
\f1\b0  (all capacity-bracket deferrals: subset peak 6.1-7.0 + reserve 1.1-1.6 > 7.20). The probation mechanism's 24-seat random figure therefore does 
\f0\b not
\f1\b0  survive equal-accounting integration: its gain is consumed by the background-reserve term (which the probation-only decision omitted), and in the aligned phase the probation-priced candidates stop earlier than the class-prior-priced chain (6 vs 8). The unified operating point of the complete system remains 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 ours_full
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333  (random 16 / aligned 8); the three measured points (24/8 probation-only upper bound, 14/6 integrated, 16/8 full chain) and the accounting difference behind them are reported as measured, and reconciling candidate pricing with the reserve is future work. 
\f0\b Why the integrated stop is lower (analysis).
\f1\b0  Two accounting differences explain the drop. (i) The background-reserve term (1.56-1.62 cores against the 7.20-core budget) is charged in the integrated runs but absent from the probation-only decision; at the defer points the subset peak (6.15/6.03) plus reserve exceeds 7.20 while the same peak without reserve is admissible - the reserve alone accounts for the gap. (ii) Candidate pricing differs roughly 3x between conventions: probation prices each candidate by its own corrected measured profile (about 0.6-0.75 cores per aligned candidate at the stop point), while the full chain charges the class prior (about 0.2 cores); the integrated configuration inherits the stricter convention. Reconciling the two (e.g., capping probation-derived candidate billing at the class prior, or explicitly folding the reserve into candidate pricing) and re-validating at equal accounting is future work.\
