\f0\b\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Replication (Figure 15 right):
\f1\b0  three rounds each (r1\'96r3). All five hot-cluster gaps of the aligned rounds hit exactly 60 s; random gaps scatter over 20\'9640 s with no fixed beat. The two "damage signatures"(periodic spikes vs baseline lift) reproduce across repeats.\

\f0\b Reading.
\f1\b0  The two phases hurt differently: synchronization is a 
\f2\i predictable periodic spike
\f1\i0  (the whole group contending inside each 60 s window); randomization 
\f2\i lifts the baseline
\f1\i0 . This refines Section 4.2 (spikes are the signature of synchronization, not a monotone "sync is worse" ordering) and motivates time-aligned admission: periodic structure can be measured, exploited (avoid peaks) or treated (anti-phase arrangement), while random structure can only be absorbed by headroom.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

