Figure 16 Sell capacity on the physical host: customer experience and service side\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \strokec2 Safety definition (unified, pre-declared, applies to all chapters).
\f1\b0  Every capacity statement is a triple of resource, quality constraint and risk budget epsilon. (i) Service side: with window W in [30,120] s, a violation is window P99 > 10 ms 
\f0\b or
\f1\b0  any timeout/error; failures are not excluded, and statements read "violation-window rate <= epsilon" with independent-round counts and Clopper-Pearson bounds (Section 5.3.2). The epsilon budget counts the 
\f2\i rate
\f1\i0  of violating rounds across an ensemble; it never weakens the within-round bar - a configuration reported as safe at epsilon must meet the customer P5 >= 70% gate in at least (1 - epsilon) of its rounds, and the epsilon = 1 endpoint of the capacity-risk curve is a terminus, not a safe-admission point. (ii) Customer side: the primary bar is customer work-rate P5 >= 70% of the solo baseline, a lower-tail quantile, not a "worst decile". (iii) Capacity oracle: the largest step satisfying both at a given epsilon. Knee (mean cliff), zero-violation capacity (epsilon = 0) and risk-budgeted capacity (epsilon > 0) are three distinct notions, and every mention says which. Timeouts and errors always count as violations, never excluded to polish the successful-request P99.\

\f0\b Question.
\f1\b0  On an 8-core tenant pool with isolated services, under "service P99 \uc0\u8804  10 ms AND customer experience not degraded", how many 1-vCPU VPS can be sold?\

\f0\b Quality bars (pre-declared, to prevent post-hoc selection).
\f1\b0  Primary bar = 
\f0\b service P99 \uc0\u8804  10 ms AND customer workout P5 \u8805  70% of the solo baseline
\f1\b0  , i.e., the low tail \'97 the 5th percentile customer keeps\uc0\u8805 70% of solo; note P5 is a lower-tail quantile, not "the worst decile". Sensitivity bars P5 \u8805  90% /P5 \u8805  50% / median P50 \u8805  90% are reported side by side on the same data. Statistics: quantiles are estimated after aggregating per customer and per round (no pooling of per-customer-per-time samples as iid); round-level uncertainty is resampled by round.\

\f0\b Method.
\f1\b0  Step ladder: L8\uc0\u8594 L64 (random group) / L4\u8594 L32 (aligned group); each step collects 60 s: pool busy (2 s), 
\f0\b per-customer burner rate
\f1\b0  (10 s), both service probes. Rates are normalized by the solo single-core baseline (25.9 M it/s); the random group pools 2 rounds (r1+r2, 13\'96117 per-customer samples), the aligned group 
\f0\b 2 rounds (r1+r2; the shuffled-order r3 is void due to a level-labeling defect and kept for the record)
\f1\b0 .\

