\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 8.6 Phase-Profile Convolution and the Marginal Illusion (Figure 28)\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs25\fsmilli12667 \cf3 \strokec3 \
Figure 28 Phase-Profile Convolution: iid blind vs PPC varying with phase structure\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 \strokec2 Proposition (marginal illusion).
\f1\b0  The two experiments of Section 8.4 are a 
\f2\i constructive proof
\f1\i0 : two fleets with 
\f0\b identical per-tenant demand marginals
\f1\b0  (same burner, same 20%/60 s duty and window shape) differ in safe capacity by 3.5x at the primary bar (28 vs 8; 40 vs 10 at the median bar). Hence 
\f0\b any capacity method that consumes only per-tenant marginals (plus an independence assumption) cannot distinguish the two fleets
\f1\b0  \'97 for AUDIBLE-style independent convolution of marginals this is a provable blind spot: the input is non-identifiable on exactly the axis that matters. This is the capacity-side analogue of the Section 3.3.1 estimation non-identifiability.\

\f0\b Method (PPC).
\f1\b0  Inputs are per-tenant phase profiles at one-second or one-minute resolution. PPC has three modes, each tied to a concrete artifact. The first is parametric simulation (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 ppc_sim.py
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 ): synthetic (phi, duty) configurations in which the joint structure holds by construction, used for mixed-phase validation and extrapolation. The second is the empirical joint block bootstrap (
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 ppc_bootstrap.py
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 ) on real traces with a strict three-way split: the first third trains, the middle third selects the block length by KS distance (B=1), and the last third is used for scoring only. Predictive blocks are drawn from the training third alone, never from validation or test, so this is not a self-resampling of the test segment. Blocks are resampled jointly across tenants to preserve co-occurrence, with an iid-shuffle control that keeps marginals and destroys the joint. Forward prediction scored against the test third: joint predicted peaks P50/P95/max = 14/14/14 with P(peak>8)=99%, iid predicted 12/13/16 with P=100%, and the observed test peak is 12 with 8% of seconds above the pool. On this production schedule both predictors get the risk direction right, and the minute-level joint-vs-iid gap is small, consistent with the fleet's drifting-phase profile; the extreme-phase evidence lives in the synthetic experiments. 
\f0\b Scope:
\f1\b0  inputs are one-minute production duties plus an in-period activity model and contain no sub-minute phase structure; this supports a minute-granularity replay model, not measured sub-minute structure. Real-trace measurements over the 64-VM x 30-min replay schedules: joint peak 14 cores (P50=P95=max=14) versus iid peaks 12-15. On this low-correlation drifting fleet the two differ little, and the iid risk grows with phase alignment (extremes in C5 and the synchronized sells). Block-length selection chose B=1 with KS~0.16; the spread across B in \{1,2,5,10,15\} is 0.16-0.21, weakly resolvable. The online replay (replay1) drove the 64-tenant schedule for 30 minutes on the 8-core pool: mean pool busy 42.0% (3.4 cores), peak saturation 100% (>=8 cores concurrent), service P99 0.706-0.729 ms across five probes with one 1.599 ms tail. The predicted over-pool risk materializes at peak while the isolation envelope keeps the service flat. The multi-window recheck sliced the same telemetry into five non-overlapping 30-minute windows, each with an independent forward prediction: direction hit 5/5 (predicted P(peak>8)=98-100% versus observed over-pool 3.5-8.3%), peak magnitudes of the same order (predicted P50 11-19 versus observed 12-16; per-window offsets +5/\'b11/-2/-1/0, variance reported). 
\f0\b Scope:
\f1\b0  the second-level series is reconstructed from minute-level duties plus a within-period phase model; this validates model-replay consistency, not independently measured second-level production truth. Both methods predict over-pool on this deliberately low-correlation fleet, so the discrimination evidence lives in the synthetic configurations (iid blind versus PPC separating five structures) and the online comparison (util80/audible collapsing in sync); independent second-level truth, false-positive/negative rates and multi-threshold behavior are future work. The third mode is the online actuator: a Taligned-style peak envelope (60 one-second phase buckets plus a class prior), a low-cost online approximation of PPC rather than a resampler; its comparison behavior (Section 7.4.1) is the evidence for that approximation. All three share one principle \'97 accounting on the joint phase structure \'97 at different resolutions and for different purposes, and the paper labels them separately.\

\f0\b Results (Figure 28).
\f1\b0  With identical parameters: iid convolution predicts 
\f0\b the same 24 for all five structures
\f1\b0  (total blindness); PPC predicts 
\f0\b 24 / 16 / 13 / 12 / 10
\f1\b0  for rand / mix25 / mix50 / mix75 /sync, with 4/5 falling inside the measured 
\f2\i coarse
\f1\i0  brackets. 
\f0\b Caveat:
\f1\b0  falling inside a bracket is not hitting the exact oracle \'97 e.g. the sync prediction of 10 sits exactly at the fine-scan first-fail step (boundary [8,10): 8 seats P5=98.8%, 10 seats P5=62.2%) \'97 the online peak gate, not the offline prediction, is what stops at 8; rand 24 is conservative by 4 after the fine scan pinned the bracket at [28,32). The random-end tail optimism matches Section 8.5 and is corrected by the measured bar.\

\f0\b Prediction-quality metrics (stricter than "inside the bracket").
\f1\b0  Per configuration: 
\f0\b over-admission
\f1\b0 (prediction >= first-fail; a safety veto, must be 0), 
\f0\b unverified stretch
\f1\b0  (prediction - last-pass; >0 means the prediction reaches into unsurveyed steps), 
\f0\b fail margin
\f1\b0  (first-fail - prediction), 
\f0\b conservatism
\f1\b0 (last-pass - prediction). Multi-round values: rand 24 vs [28,32) \'97 over 0, conservative 4; mix25 16 vs [24,28) \'97 over 0, conservative 8; mix50 13 vs [13,16) \'97 over 0, margin 3; mix75 12 vs [10,13) \'97 over 0, stretch +2, margin 1; sync 10 vs [8,10) \'97 
\f0\b over 1 (first-fail), vetoed online
\f1\b0 . 
\f0\b 4/5 zero over-admission
\f1\b0 ; the sync case is exactly why the online peak gate \'97 not the offline predictor \'97 carries the safety argument.\

\f0\b Boundaries.
\f1\b0  PPC needs phase history (cold start degrades to priors/observation windows); the random end still needs the measured bar; validation is offline forward prediction (train-only resampling scored on the held-out third) plus the online comparison (the 
\f3\fs28\fsmilli14107 ours
\f1\fs30\fsmilli15333 policy 
\f2\i is
\f1\i0  the PPC actuator and stops on the synchronized oracle).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

