\f1\fs28 ; reruns in seconds, no external dependencies).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 \strokec2 A.1 Proposition A (censoring impossibility)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Model.
\f1\b0  A single cgroup with bandwidth quota (period $P$, budget $qP$), observation window $W = P$; observed quantities $(u, t)$.\

\f0\b Construction.
\f1\b0  $q = 0.2$. Process A: $d \\equiv 5/9$; process B: $d = 1.0$ for 84% of the period, 0 otherwise.
\f2\i A:
\f1\i0  the budget exhausts after $(q/d)P = 0.36P$; $t = 1 - q/d = 0.64$, $u = q = 0.2$, mean $5/9$.
\f2\i B:
\f1\i0  inside the active stretch the budget exhausts after $0.2P$; throttling continues until the active stretch ends ($0.84P$); $t = 0.64$, $u = 0.2$, mean $0.84$. Hence $(u,t) = (0.2, 0.64)$ for 
\f0\b both
\f1\b0  processes while the means differ by $0.284$: no estimator that sees only the aggregate counters can be correct for both. This is an 
\f0\b identifiability
\f1\b0  impossibility \'97it holds for any deterministic function of the observations \'97 and is stronger than statistical unbiasedness. \uc0\u8718 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 A.2 Proposition B1 (capacity is not determined by marginals)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Construction.
\f1\b0  $N$ tenants, each active for $w$ seconds per period (identical per-tenant marginals), pool $C = 8$, $\\rho = w/P$. Aligned: peak concurrency $= N$, so the capacity is exactly $C$. Random: the mean concurrency is$\\rho N$, and any reasonable safety bar requires the time-average not to overload, i.e. $\\rho N \\le C$; hence $N \\le C/\\rho$ is an 
\f0\b upper bound
\f1\b0  (peak constraints only tighten it). Therefore the capacity ratio is 
\f0\b at most
\f1\b0  $1/\\rho$ (i.e. 5 at $\\rho = 0.2$) for the 
\f0\b zero-loss
\f1\b0  notion. 
\f0\b Scope of the bound.
\f1\b0  This bound assumes zero performance loss: it requires the time-average delivered demand not to overload the pool ($\\rho N \\le C$). The operational bar used in this paper (customer work-rate P5 $\\ge$ 70% of the solo baseline) explicitly permits QoE degradation, so the measured primary-bar capacities are a different, bar-dependent object; $C/\\rho$ bounds only the zero-loss notion and is cited as such. The measured primary-bar capacities are 28 vs 8 (3.5x) \'97 inside that bound (~70% of it); stricter tail bars shrink the ratio further. \uc0\u8718 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 A.3 Proposition B2 (threshold impossibility; theorem, any window length)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Theorem.
\f1\b0  For an aligned fleet, the window-mean delivered utilization over any window of length $L$(possibly spanning multiple periods) is at most$U(L) = \\big[w\\lfloor L/P \\rfloor + \\min(L \\bmod P, w)\\big]/L$.\

\f0\b Proof.
\f1\b0  The activity set is periodic with period $P$ and $w$ active seconds per period. Any$L$-second window contains $\\lfloor L/P \\rfloor$ full periods (each contributing $w$ seconds of overlap) plus one residual window of length $r = L \\bmod P$ (overlap at most $\\min(r, w)$). Delivery rate is at most $C$, so the window utilization is at most $U(L)$. \uc0\u8718 \

\f0\b Special cases and corollary.
\f1\b0  For $L \\le P$, $U(L) = \\min(w,L)/L$ (the 30-s rolling window used by util80: $12/30 = 40\\%$); for $L$ an integer multiple of $P$, $U = \\rho$; for $L = kP + r$ with $r > 0$,$U$ can slightly exceed $\\rho$ (e.g. $L = 90$ s: $24/90 = 26.7\\%$). Any rule "admit while windowed utilization $< \\theta$" with $\\theta > U(L)$ admits unbounded $N$ on aligned fleets whose safe capacity is only $C$ (util80: $40\\% < \\theta = 80\\%$; it sold to the experiment cap of 40 \'97 Section 7.4.1).\

\f0\b Executable checks.
\f1\b0  T-B3 (single-period caps) and T-B4 (multi-period formula verified numerically by sliding windows; the earlier single-period formula is demonstrably wrong for $L \\in \\\{90, 120, 150\\\}$).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 A.4 Proposition C (sampling insufficiency)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs30\fsmilli15333 \cf2 Construction.
\f1\b0  Sampler interval $\\Delta$, burst width $w < \\Delta$, burst window placed between two samples (e.g. $\\Delta = 30$ s, sample phase 3 s, burst $[15, 27)$): the bursty fleet and a benign (all-idle) fleet produce 
\f0\b identical
\f1\b0  all-zero sample sequences. Positive control: at $\\Delta = 10$ s a sample necessarily lands inside the burst. \uc0\u8718 
\f0\b Corollary.
\f1\b0  The sampling rate must be at least $1/w$ (this paper's online history uses 1 s for 12-s bursts \'97 a 12x margin).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf2 A.5 Scope of the formal results\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\qj\partightenfactor0

